  "body": "AFAICT, various JS libraries would stop working if they would have to adhere to the currently stated restrictions on children of document.documentElement or [`<html>`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#the-html-element):\r\n\r\n> Content model:\r\n> A `head` element followed by a `body` element.\r\n\r\nBoth W3c's and WHATWG's [spec for HTML says](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/dom.html#content-models):\r\n\r\n> An HTML element must have contents that match the requirements described in the element's content model. The contents of an element are its children in the DOM.\r\n\r\n> Authors must not use HTML elements anywhere except where they are explicitly allowed, as defined for each element, or as explicitly required by other specifications.\r\n\r\nSome scenarios would be even impossible to implement (at least reliably) e.g. chrome/firefox extensions working at so called `document_start` moment when DOM has only a childless documentElement, to which they append an inline `script` element with _synchronously_ running code to modify/extend the page in some way before any of its scripts run.\r\n\r\nP.S. DOM spec says [`document element` is an `Element`](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#document-trees) so it [can contain any `Element`](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#node-trees) inside.",
